Here is an example of how to generate a table of contents (TOC) in a LaTeX document using the `toc` package:

```
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath, amssymb}

% Define the TOC command
\newcommand{\toc}[1]{%
  \setcounter{section}{0}%
  \setcounter{tocdepth}{#1}%
  \tableofcontents%
}

% Example usage
\begin{document}
\maketitle

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}

This document provides an overview of some topics related to my research.

\section{Methodology}
\label{sec:methodology}

In this section, I will describe the methodology used for my research.

\section{Results}
\label{sec:result}

I have presented some results from my research in this section.

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

In this section, I will discuss the findings and their implications.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

In conclusion, I will summarize the main points of my research and provide recommendations for future work.

\end{document}
```

The `\toc` command takes two arguments: the number of sections (`#1`) and the depth of the TOC (`#2`). In this example, we set the depth to 3 (`#2=3`) so that there are three levels of headings in the TOC.

To use the TOC, simply call `\toc` at the beginning of each section or subsection, followed by the desired level of heading (`\label{}`). For instance,

```
\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:introduction}
\begin{subsubsection}{Sub-subsection}
    This is a sub-subsection.
\end{subsubsection}
\begin{subsubsection}{Sub-subsection}
    Another sub-subsection.
\end{subsubsection}
\end{document}
```

will produce a TOC with three headings (`\label{}`), one for each sub-subsection.

